"The e-positivity and Schur positivity of some spiders and broom trees - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0166218X22003869)\nciteturn10search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; This section is devoted to the e-positivity and Schur positivity of the spiders S(a,b,1). ... If the spider S(a,b,c) is e-positive, then a=b+c.\nA strong motivation for studying the positivity of chromatic symmetric functions is Stanley and Stembridge’s conjecture posed in 1993, see Conjecture 1.1. A graph is claw-free if it does not contain an induced subgraph which is isomorphic to the claw. The incomparability graph of a poset P is the graph with vertex set P, in which two elements are adjacent if and only if they are incomparable.\n\nConjecture 1.1 Stanley and Stembridge [38]\n\nAny claw-free incomparability graph is e-positive.\n\nWolfgang III [42] provided a powerful criterion that any connected e-positive graph has a connected partition of every type, where a connected partition of a graph G is a partition \\{{V}_{1},\\dots ,{V}_{k}\\} of V\\left(G\\right) such that each induced subgraph G\\left[{V}_{i}\\right] is connected. Many graph classes are known to be e-positive, including complete graphs, paths, cycles, triad-free graphs, generalized bull graphs, (claw, {K}_{3})-free graphs, (claw, co-{P}_{3})-free graphs, F-free unit interval graphs for all 4-vertex graphs F except the co-diamond, {K}_{4}, 4{K}_{1} and 2{K}_{2}, (claw, paw)-free graphs, (claw, co-paw)-free graphs, (claw, diamond, co-diamond)-free graphs, (claw, triangle)-free graphs, (claw, co-{P}_{3})-free graphs, (claw, co-diamond, 2{K}_{2})-free graphs, 2{K}_{2}-free unit interval graphs, K-chains, lollipop graphs, triangular ladders, (claw, co-claw)-free graph except the net, and Ferrers graphs; see [3], [4], [6], [9], [10], [11], [16], [18], [23], [35], [39]. Graphs that are proved not to be e-positive include the dart, generalized nets, saltire graphs {\\mathrm{SA}}_{n,n}, augmented saltire graphs {\\mathrm{AS}}_{n,n} and {\\mathrm{AS}}_{n,n+1}, and triangular tower graphs {\\mathrm{TT}}_{n,n,n}; see [7], [8], [11], [12]. In 2020 Dahlberg et al. [7] gave an infinite number of families of non-e-positive graphs that are not contractible to the claw. Moreover, one such family is additionally claw-free, thus establishing that the e-positivity is in general not dependent on the existence of an induced claw or of a contraction to a claw.\n\nIn this paper, we concentrate on the chromatic symmetric functions of trees. This is not only for the simplicity of trees as a particular class of graphs, but also for the following major conjecture in this direction, which is known as Stanley’s isomorphism conjecture and the tree isomorphism conjecture, see Loebl and Sereni [24] and Crew and Spirkl [5].\n\nConjecture 1.3\n\nThe chromatic symmetric function distinguishes trees.\n\nIn fact, Conjecture 1.3 was inspired by Stanley [35, Page 170]’s remark “We do not know whether {X}_{G} distinguishes trees”. See [1], [12], [13], [17], [19], [26], [28], [29], [39] for its research progress.\n\nThe problem of determining whether a given tree is e-positive and whether it is Schur positive also received attention. Dahlberg et al. [8] conjectured the existence of an n-vertex Schur positive tree of maximum degree \\lfloor n/2\\rfloor for all n\\ge 2, which was disproved by Rambeloson and Shareshian [30] with a counterexample for n=10. Dahlberg et al. [8] also proved that the degree of any n-vertex e-positive tree is at most {log}_{2}n, and further conjectured the maximum degree of any e-positive tree to be 3.\n\nConjecture 1.4 Dahlberg et al. [8]\n\nAny tree with a vertex of degree at least 4 is not e-positive.\n\nZheng [43] obtained a breakthrough towards Conjecture 1.4 by proving that any tree with maximum degree at least 6 is not e-positive.\n\nA particular class of trees, the spiders, plays an essential role in the study of e-positivity of graphs. A spider is a tree consisting of some paths with one endpoint on each path identified. Precisely speaking, for any partition \\lambda ={\\lambda }_{1}\\cdots {\\lambda }_{d}⊢n-1 with d\\ge 3, the spider S\\left(\\lambda \\right) is the n-vertex tree consisting of paths of lengths {\\lambda }_{1},\\dots ,{\\lambda }_{d} such that all of them share a common endpoint of degree d. Dahlberg et al. [8, Lemma 13] showed that if a connected graph G has a connected partition of type \\mu , then the spider S\\left(\\lambda \\right) has a connected partition of type \\mu , where \\lambda is the partition consisting of the sizes of connected components that are obtained by removing a vertex of degree at least 3 from G. Therefore, the e-positivity of a general graph implies the e-positivity of certain spider in view of Wolfgang III [42]’s criterion.\n\n## Access through your organization\n\nCheck access to the full text by signing in through your organization.\n\n\n## Preliminaries\n\nLet \\mathbb{N}=\\{0,1,\\dots \\phantom{\\rule{0.16667em}{0ex}}\\} be the set of natural numbers and n\\in \\mathbb{N}. A composition \\alpha of n is a sequence ({\\alpha }_{1},{\\alpha }_{2},\\dots \\phantom{\\rule{0.16667em}{0ex}}) of natural numbers that sum to n, in which the numbers {\\alpha }_{i} are called parts of \\alpha . An integer partition \\lambda of n is a finite composition ({\\lambda }_{1},\\dots ,{\\lambda }_{l}) of n such that {\\lambda }_{1}\\ge \\cdots \\ge {\\lambda }_{l}\\ge 1, where l\\in \\mathbb{N}. A partition \\lambda can be denoted in the following various ways: \\lambda =({\\lambda }_{1},\\dots ,{\\lambda }_{l})={\\lambda }_{1}\\cdots {\\lambda }_{l}={1}^{{a}_{1}}\\cdots {n}^{{a}_{n}}⊢n,where {a}_{i}\\in \\mathbb{N} is the multiplicity of i. A term {i}^{{a}_{i}} is often omitted when {a}_{i}=0. It is obvious that every composition \\alpha corresponds to a\n\n## The positivity of spiders S(a,b,1)\n\nThis section is devoted to the e-positivity and Schur positivity